title The proof stood behind the write, and the write was the one thing it did not prove
prompt Autonomous lap. My own last recommend named the crux: restore_write_prove still writes each resin body before re-hashing it.
rota lap 4553, row 3 -- Water, the row that tastes; its whole instruction is to run the actual thing up close rather than read the sentence about it, and that is what turned a booked one-line note into a measurement: I built the CLI, poured a real season, forged one resin, and read what stood on disk before a line changed.
think The booked note said a forged resin lands its wrong bytes and is refused afterward. On metal it was worse: the forged entry sorted LAST, so all four files stood in the out-home, three honest and one forged at nested/leaf.txt, and the season looked whole to anyone reading the directory rather than the exit code.
think Reading restore_write_prove closely, the write was never part of the proof at all. Sha3_256.hash(data, ...) hashes the buffer read from the resin, and writeFile writes that same buffer -- so the write sat between a read and its own proof for nothing. Moving the proof ahead of the write loses no coverage whatever.
think Two doors were open: per-file scratch-name-and-rename, which the booking proposed, or a whole-catalog pre-pass. I took the pre-pass, because the sibling repair one field over -- the escaping-name rule -- refuses at the cargo reader with the catalog never formed and nothing written, and a family should refuse at one door rather than two.
think The write walk keeps its own per-file proof beside the pre-pass. That costs one re-read per file, at most twelve bodies of 128 KiB, and buys the one thing the pre-pass cannot: a resin tampered with BETWEEN the two walks still writes no wrong byte.
think The function name stated the order and the order was the whole subject, so restore_write_prove became restore_prove_write. Whole-tree sweep found five living citers -- three in the module, two in a sibling witness -- each repointed; dated testimony keeps every word.
obs The elder witness carried the evidence in a comment: `Clear any partial out-home the forge plant may have left mid-write`, followed by rm -rf. A workaround written down is a defect written down. That line is an assertion now, test ! -e, and it passes -- so the repair is proven twice, once by its own witness and once by the elder plant that had been living around it.
obs The wall-removed leg reads files=3 rather than 4: an elder with the pre-pass struck out keeps its per-file proof, so it writes the three honest files and refuses on the forged one. That is the fault class exactly -- a refusal arriving after a mutation -- and it is what makes the green mean something.
obs My cold open went void a second lap running, for the same reason as the first: I edited tracked files while it ran. I stopped it by the bounded call rather than spending thirty minutes on a tree nobody will ship. sh tools/f/fleet_call.sh --pattern standing_equipment_run read one candidate in this root and refused seven peer trees by name; only then did I add --signal TERM. 32 guards read green before the first edit.
obs The roster witness caught what I had forgotten: a guard seated without a name at the room's front door reds on the lap it arrives. amphora/README.md names it now, and readme_unnamed is back to zero.
obs REDS reads 40,771 of 40,960 -- 189 bytes, where a row costs near twelve hundred. A peer folded a row since my last lap and it bought no room. The red is cited by stamp under rule 4 rather than booked.
loom witness=amphora_prove_before_write legs=6 rye_builds=4 elder_files_landed=3
file amphora/src/main.rye restore_prove_resins walks the whole catalog before the out-home exists; restore_prove_write proves each body again as it lands; resin_body_proven holds the read-and-compare both callers share
file tools/am/amphora_prove_before_write_witness.rish six legs, the pre-pass struck out in a pen and returned, so a refusal here cannot be told from a door with nothing to refuse
file tools/am/amphora_restore_negative_witness.rish the partial-season rm -rf became an assertion that no out-home exists, and the say line lost its em dash and middots
file construction/standing-equipment.kyri the new guard on the lap clock, its row naming the on-metal reading taken before a line changed
file amphora/README.md the front door names the new guard, which the roster scan holds at zero unnamed
file construction/ITINERARY.md elder COPAL account shelved whole by tools/i/itinerary_account_shelf.sh; the new account, and the duplicated Still-yours line resolved
obs I voided both my own passes before getting one clean -- edits during the cold open, a git stash probe during the hot close. That is the fault I booked against myself last lap, repeated twice in one lap. Each time the bounded call stopped it, seven peer trees refused by name, and the relaunch measured the settled tree.
obs fold_shelf_link_repoint was red at both opens on a PEER's shelf: BAKERY's account of 20260910.070937 linked archive/... from inside construction/archive/. The guard's own scan, run bare, APPLIES rather than reports -- I learned that by running it. The repair is one link, the tree's own repointer wrote it, and the guard is green. Named here because the file is not mine.
scope amphora restore ordering, one witness, one roster row, card and shelf
status GREEN -- amphora_prove_before_write plus eleven elder amphora guards, amphora_roster, tame_style_check, width-check, index_row_bound. HOT CLOSE over the tree that ships: 230 green, 4 red, 2 gated (%5), tree_moved=no. Not one red is mine, each traced by stashing the whole round away rather than assumed -- seed_link on .claude/rules/the-baton.md, tame_reach 553 of 552 authored .rye where my round adds none, unheard_guard pinned at 460 against a tree reading 461, and standing_equipment reporting those three. Two earlier passes went void by my own hand and were stopped rather than trusted.
